{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Two roadmap entries are committed and pushed on `worktree-roadmap-scope-and-config` — but I stopped short of merging to main, for a reason you should know about.\n\n## Heads up: another session is editing your main checkout right now\n\n`README.md` and `docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md` have uncommitted changes I didn't make, written 38 seconds before I tried to merge. They're substantive — one documents the allowlist false-positive reproducing itself through cledger's *own documentation* (observed from `intent-recall`), another reports that `formatFinding` doesn't show enough context to judge a finding on a 26 KB turn (from dogfooding `turnbridge`).\n\nThe merge needs to update both files, so git refused. I could have stashed, but yanking files out from under a live process risks destroying its in-flight edits, so I left everything alone. My commit is safe on the pushed branch; your main checkout is untouched. Tell me when that session has landed and I'll merge in one step.\n\nAmusingly, that other session's finding is the same bug family as the one you just named.\n\n## 1 & 3. Per-branch — yes, that's the right unit, and no, storage isn't where it goes\n\nYour instinct about the unit is right. I want to push back on one specific thing: that storing it would be *simpler and safer*.\n\n**Branch membership is derived, not intrinsic.** One commit sits on many branches at once, and merge, rebase, cherry-pick, rename, and delete all change which ones — without touching the commit. Anchoring to commits leaves that derivation where git already maintains it. Storing per-branch denormalizes it, and then you own the synchronization on every one of those operations. Git itself works this way: it doesn't store \"which branches contain this commit,\" `git branch --contains` computes it. A stored copy is a cache, and caches go stale.\n\nThe decisive case is your own workflow. **Squash merge deletes the source branch.** A per-branch ledger ref would go with it, taking the conversations. That's exactly the scenario re-anchoring exists to survive, and per-branch storage would reintroduce it in a worse form — because with commit anchoring the events survive the branch's deletion and can be re-anchored, whereas a deleted ref is just gone.\n\nSo \"per branch shown, not stored\" isn't the flimsy version. It's derived-on-read from the structure git guarantees, rather than a copy someone has to remember to update.\n\n## 2. Your critique of the export fix is correct, and sharper than I gave it credit for\n\nYou're right that flipping `export`'s default papers over it. And your reason is the good one: **future consumers won't go through `export`.** `intent-recall` will call `readEvents` as a library, not shell out to a CLI. So putting the scoping in one command puts it in the wrong layer entirely — the next consumer bypasses it and gets the unscoped view again.\n\nThat changes my recommendation. The default belongs in `readEvents` itself, with an explicit opt-out, so every consumer inherits it whether they come through the CLI or the library. Export just becomes one caller among many.\n\nBut that's still only the read half. It does nothing about what leaves your machine, which is the part you actually flagged.\n\n## The notes question — you're partly right, and it's localized\n\nAre we fighting notes? On one axis, yes: **one ref is one push unit**, and that's precisely where distribution granularity gets welded to storage granularity. That friction is real.\n\nBut it's fixable *within* notes, which is why I don't think the model is wrong. Keep storage as it is, and make distribution per-branch by pushing the reachable subset to `refs/notes/conversation-ledger-branches/<branch>`, with a wildcard fetch refspec union-merging them back into one local ref. A collaborator who never fetches your branch never receives its conversations. Storage stays commit-anchored and derivation-free; distribution becomes branch-scoped, which is the thing you actually share.\n\nOne trap worth recording, which is why the obvious version doesn't work: you can't just push a *filtered* view of the shared ref. Push is a ref update, not a merge — pushing a subset would delete other branches' events from the remote's tree. Per-branch remote refs avoid the clobber.\n\nOn the separate-repo idea: it relocates the problem rather than dissolving it. You'd lose object sharing (so the ledger stops being GC-safe alongside the code), need your own transport and merge strategy, and still face \"which branch owns this conversation\" — the same question, now without git's DAG to answer it. Worth noting sticky-note already does roughly that shape (orphan `sticky-note/data` branch plus a notes ref), so it isn't an unexplored escape hatch.\n\n## 4. Added\n\nBoth are in the roadmap on that branch, with the config bug written up including why it fails open and that your global `knownSecrets: true` is what it would silently disable. I also named the pattern you spotted — cledger's own state feeding back into cledger's behavior is now a recurring family: the capture/scan redaction loop, the worktree state split, this config merge, and the other session's documentation-reproduces-the-false-positive finding. That's four. It probably deserves a standing check rather than four separate fixes.\n\nneeds input: main has uncommitted edits from a concurrent session, so I left `worktree-roadmap-scope-and-config` (pushed, commit `29efcd2`) unmerged — say when that session has landed and I'll merge it.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","dirty_fingerprint":"bbe20560a71a254635cbd033aa353a00fbd2c3fb094525003f36b4423871768b","head":"183e3e0941068e8524d568b98297e3439d058c1f","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:cef69ec2-8b5a-4e91-8078-ac955e039295","seq"